// # X86-SEC — hostile fold, leftover println, and secret-sink refusal audit
//
// Logline: emit-x86 of fixtures/x86_fold_hostile.oo, x86_leftover_ident.oo, and any secret-tainted println must ERR with x86_emit and not emit a successful _start that omitted the extra print or leaked the secret.
//
// Setup: cwd is ooda/. Host is x86_64. x86_emit_stream runs check_secret_println_sinks before any match or harvest arm. .oo file law, 256-line cap, and C emit + gcc are off-limits.
//
// Beats:
//   1. Static read of x86_emit.oo to confirm the secret check runs first.
//   2. Confirm the two adversarial smokes exist on disk and what each locks.
//   3. State the x86_secret_smoke.oo gap; SEC is partial.
//   4. Hostile-proof: hello.oo fold is RBRACE-tight, but a second println must ERR.
//   5. Leftover-ident: let message = greet(...); println(message) folds to a call and must ERR.
//   6. Secret taint: a //secret println must process_exit before any emit.

// ## 0. Verdict
//
// hostile-plus-print: PASS (smoke exists, asserts ERR + no _start)
// leftover-ident:     PASS (smoke exists, asserts ERR + no _start)
// secret-sink refusal: UNPROVEN (x86_secret_smoke.oo is not on disk)
//
// No CRIT. No HIGH. Did not edit product .oo or fixtures. Did not rebuild oodac.

// ## 1. Secret check runs before any match or harvest arm
//
// File: ooda/oodac/x86_emit.oo
//
//   1:  // # Emit Linux x86_64 AT&T assembly
//   ...
//   8:  //   1. Tokenize and refuse secret sinks.
//  16:  import "c_emit_secret_check.oo";
//  25:  pub fn x86_emit_stream(src: String) {
//  30:      let toks: List[String] = tokenize_lines(src);
//  31:      check_secret_println_sinks(src, toks);
//  32:      let fold: String = x86_fold_hello(src);
//
// The .oo header beat 1 (line 8) is "Tokenize and refuse secret
// sinks.". Line 16 imports the host C-emit secret check; line 31
// calls it. The call sits BEFORE the fold on line 32, the match
// arms on lines 39-58, and the harvest on line 59. A secret-tainted
// println must process_exit from check_secret_println_sinks before
// any .s is written.
//
// c_emit_secret_check.oo is owned by C; x86_emit.oo is on the
// forbidden list for C lane edits, so SEC only audits that the
// call is wired and runs first.

// ## 2. Two adversarial smokes on disk (hostile + leftover)
//
// File: ooda/scripts/x86_hostile_leftover_smoke.oo
//
//   1:  // # emit-x86 leftover-plus-print honesty lock
//   ...
//   3:  // Logline: emit-x86 of fixtures/x86_fold_hostile.oo prints ERR x86_emit and does not emit a silent omit.
//  32:      let src = root + "/fixtures/x86_fold_hostile.oo";
//  37:      if process_exec(p, oodac + " emit-x86 " + src + " > " + sfile + " 2>" + serr).is_ok() {
//  38:          if expect_clean(p, "grep -q _start " + sfile).is_ok() {
//  39:              fail("successful .s omitted leftover println");
//  40:          }
//  41:      }
//  42:      if expect_clean(p, "grep -qE 'ERR' " + sfile + " " + serr).is_err() { fail("no ERR"); }
//  43:      if expect_clean(p, "grep -q x86_emit " + sfile + " " + serr).is_err() { fail("no x86_emit"); }
//  44:      if expect_clean(p, "! grep -q _start " + sfile).is_err() { fail("silent omit .s"); }
//
// Latches the hostile case: emit-x86 must ERR, must mention
// x86_emit, and must NOT emit a _start .s that quietly dropped the
// extra print.
//
// File: ooda/scripts/x86_leftover_ident_smoke.oo
//
//   1:  // # emit-x86 leftover ident honesty lock
//   ...
//   3:  // Logline: emit-x86 of fixtures/x86_leftover_ident.oo prints ERR x86_emit and does not emit a silent omit.
//  32:      let src = root + "/fixtures/x86_leftover_ident.oo";
//  37:      if process_exec(p, oodac + " emit-x86 " + src + " > " + sfile + " 2>" + serr).is_ok() {
//  38:          if expect_clean(p, "grep -q _start " + sfile).is_ok() {
//  39:              fail("successful .s omitted leftover println");
//  40:          }
//  41:      }
//  42:      if expect_clean(p, "grep -qE 'ERR' " + sfile + " " + serr).is_err() { fail("no ERR"); }
//  43:      if expect_clean(p, "grep -q x86_emit " + sfile + " " + serr).is_err() { fail("no x86_emit"); }
//  44:      if expect_clean(p, "! grep -q _start " + sfile).is_err() { fail("silent omit .s"); }
//
// Latches the let-bind case: even when bind_names is populated, a
// println(<ident-that-is-a-call>) must ERR. The phalanx §0 disk-now
// file x86_phalanx.oot line 13 names "let/mut" as harvested, so the
// lock is targeted at the real failure mode the harvest must not
// silently absorb.
//
// Fixture presence (verified at audit time, not run):
//   ooda/fixtures/x86_fold_hostile.oo     present
//   ooda/fixtures/x86_leftover_ident.oo  present

// ## 3. SEC smoke gap: x86_secret_smoke.oo does not exist
//
// x86_phalanx.oot lines 99-100:
//
//    99:  SEC   ooda/scripts/x86_secret_smoke.oo
//   100:        openOODA/audit/x86_sec.oot
//
// The x86_phalanx contract names x86_secret_smoke.oo as the SEC
// smoke. On disk, that file is NOT present:
//
//   $ ls ooda/scripts/x86_secret_smoke.oo
//   ls: cannot access '...': No such file or directory
//
// x86_emit.oo line 31 calls check_secret_println_sinks on every
// emit. A secret-tainted println that lands in a fold arm, a match
// arm, or the harvest will exit before any .s. But no smoke asserts
// this for the x86 lane. A silent regression in
// check_secret_println_sinks would not be caught by any x86 smoke
// until the C lane caught it.
//
// Status: x86_secret_smoke.oo is OPEN. The contract says it should
// own a fixture like fixtures/x86_secret_print.oo whose top
// declares //secret on a string and whose body is println(<that>).
// emit-x86 must process_exit on ERR before any .s lines.

// ## 4. Hostile-proof on disk (fold shape, RBRACE-tight)
//
// x86_phalanx.oot §10 handoff line 187:
//   "Do not loosen hello fold RBRACE-tight shape."
//
// File: ooda/oodac/x86_emit_fold.oo
//
//  177:  pub fn x86_fold_hello(src: String) -> String {
//  ...
//  183:      let parts: List[String] = x86_greet_parts(toks, n);
//  184:      if list_len(parts) < 2 {
//  185:          return "";
//  186:      }
//  187:      let arg: String = x86_main_greet_arg(toks, n);
//  191:      return list_get(parts, 0) + arg + list_get(parts, 1);
//  192:  }
//
// x86_greet_parts (x86_emit_fold.oo:67-110) requires:
//   - greet fn found
//   - return STRING PLUS IDENT PLUS STRING where IDENT is the param
//     name at lines 88-98
//   - RBRACE closes the fn body at lines 99-104
//
// x86_main_greet_arg (x86_emit_fold.oo:112-175) requires:
//   - main fn found
//   - let IDENT = greet(STRING) at lines 122-147
//   - println(IDENT) at lines 155-166
//   - RBRACE closes main at lines 167-173
//
// Any extra println in main before or after this block is not in
// the matched token window, so the fold returns "". emit-x86 then
// falls through to the harvest in x86_emit.oo:59. The harvest's
// leftover_unresolved flag is set when an unresolved println is
// seen, and x86_emit_walk.oo:205-208 prints
// "ERR\tx86_emit\tleftover println(ident)" and process_exit(1).
// That is the hostile lock.

// ## 5. Leftover-ident path on disk
//
// File: ooda/oodac/x86_emit_walk.oo
//
//  201:      if !saw_main {
//  202:          println("ERR\tx86_emit\tresidual: need fn main");
//  203:          process_exit(1);
//  204:      }
//  205:      if leftover_unresolved {
//  206:          println("ERR\tx86_emit\tleftover println(ident)");
//  207:          process_exit(1);
//  208:      }
//
// leftover_unresolved is set on lines 28-30 (KW_WHILE / KW_FOR),
// 102, 119, 129, 136, 140, 147, 158, 161, 176, 179, 192 (mix,
// alu, call, and ident misses).
//
// The let-bind + println case from x86_leftover_ident_smoke.oo
// lands in the println(IDENT) branch (x86_emit_walk.oo:163-181).
// mix_try runs and fails. alu_try runs and fails. The trailing
// RPAREN check on line 171 fires, x86_bind_idx on int_names
// misses, leftover_unresolved becomes true, ERR prints,
// process_exit(1).

// ## 6. Secret taint path on disk (not yet smoke-locked)
//
// File: ooda/oodac/c_emit_secret_check.oo
//
//  17:  pub fn check_secret_println_sinks(src: String, toks: List[String]) {
//  18:      let mut env: String = c_parse_secret_env(src);
//  20:      env = c_secret_mark_returning_fns(src, toks, env);
//  21:      if env == "" {
//  22:          return;
//  ...
//  66:          if k == "IDENT" {
//  67:              let id: String = c_tokt(toks, i);
//  69:              let sid: String = c_secret_resolve_alias(env, id);
//  70:              if sid == "println" || sid == "eprintln" {
//  ...
//  99:                  c_secret_refuse_sink_resolved(env, sid, full_path);
//
// Lines 70 and 99 are the trap: a taint that resolves to println
// walks each IDENT arg, builds the dot path, and calls
// c_secret_refuse_sink_resolved. c_emit_secret_check.oo is not in
// the x86 SEC ownership set, but the call site at x86_emit.oo:31
// is. SEC needs a smoke that:
//   - takes a fixture whose top marks a string as //secret
//   - has main do println(<that>)
//   - runs oodac emit-x86 on it
//   - asserts ERR on stdout or stderr
//   - asserts no _start line in .s
// The fixture and the smoke do not exist.

